3 eggs in the morning = 3/12 dozen eggs because 1 dozen = 12 eggs. So, if Claire eats 3 eggs every day, the number of eggs she eats in a day can be converted to dozens as follows:

\boxed{Daily Eggs} = \boxed{\frac{Daily\, Eggs}{12}} \boxed{Dozen\, Eggs}

Now we have: \boxed{Daily\, Eggs} = \boxed{\frac{3\, Eggs}{12\, Eggs}} = \boxed{\frac{1}{4} Dozen}

Since there are 7 days in a week, the total number of eggs she eats in a week is:

\boxed{Weekly\, Eggs} = \boxed{Daily\, Eggs} \boxed{× \, Weeks\, per\, Day} = \boxed{\frac{1}{4} Dozen} \boxed{× \, 7\, Days\, per\, Week} = \boxed{\frac{7}{12}\, Dozens}

Now, we have 4 weeks in a month. So, the total number of eggs she eats in a month is:

\boxed{Monthly\, Eggs} = \boxed{Weekly\, Eggs} \boxed{× \, Months\, per\, Week} = \boxed{\frac{7}{12} Dozen\, Eggs} \boxed{× \, 4\, Weeks\, per\, Month} = \boxed{\frac{28}{36} Dozen\, Eggs}

Converting back to mixed numbers, we have:

\boxed{\frac{28}{36} Dozen} ≈ \boxed{0} \boxed{3} \boxed{Period\, like\,\dfrac{1}{3} Dozen}

So, Claire eats about 0.3 dozen plus some fraction of an egg dozen in 1 month. If we round this to the nearest whole number, she eats approximately:

\boxed{answer} = \boxed{0} \boxed{3} \boxed{Dozen\, Eggs}

